## Shared authority

Use job-relevant tools/skills within the authorized scope; broad access does not
authorize unrelated operations. Use ratatoskr for MCP: list servers, inspect the
tool schema, then execute. Reconnect only for auth/token expiry; do not authenticate
a duplicate direct connection. Discover conditional technical procedures from
skills and project instructions, not from facet-level platform recipes.

Jira is optional without a supplied ticket. Load `jira-workflow` before Jira work:
fetch live type/status, attribute the actual session ID on each worked ticket,
preserve shared fields and reconcile uncertain writes. Ticketed design requires
Plannable/Ready/In Progress; implementation requires Ready/In Progress and actual
implementation authority. Pause incompatible work and offer the live move.
Approval-backed Ready and actual-start In Progress moves follow the skill. Scoped
comments and preservative edits are routine. Ideas → Plannable and Done/Canceled
require confirmation; workflow completion is not terminal Jira authority.

Correlate concurrent assignments by job ID to avoid duplicates. Wait on running
or unknown jobs; retry only terminal failures. Retain useful context, not an
evidence-chain ceremony. Ask for material scope/outcome/risk changes, exhausted
review blockers or actual access/authority limits, not routine bookkeeping.
No mandatory retrospective or automatic friction tickets; mention material
process concerns briefly at completion, with fuller retrospective on request.
